  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1014 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nTHIS WEEK:\n\nThe current synoptic level setup continues to be dominated by high \npressure over the west. Beginning on Monday, moisture and PWAT \nanomalies begin to take a downturn compared to previous days. This \nwill mean less widespread precipitation chances for the area. \nHowever, there remains enough moisture to cause daily afternoon \nthunderstorm chances over the higher terrain. Orographic lift will \nalso be a contributing factor to help enhance these mountain storm \nchances. Additionally, morning cloud cover has the ability to \ndictate where storm activity materializes as daytime heating can \naffect the evolution of storms. As a result of all of this, \nlocalized heavy rainfall is possible from these storms which \ninherently poses a threat of flash flooding, especially around burn \nscars and susceptible terrain. \n\nBy midweek, the high gradually shifts back towards the Desert \nSouthwest. Because of the continuous presence of moisture, each\nafternoon's storm chances may look similar to each other. That \nis, isolated to scattered and mostly confined to the higher \nterrain. Synoptic forcing will be somewhat weak for this period,\nwith the occasional shortwave interrupting the flow around the \nhigh, which will provide some additional lift. So long as the \nsurface levels remain saturated in certain places, flash \nflooding will remain a concern, particularly for burn scars and \nsusceptible terrain. Primary threats with these storms continue \nto be localized heavy rainfall, lightning, and gusty outflows. \n\nDaytime highs will start off ~5 degrees above normal to start\nthe week, with a slight uptick to ~5-10 degrees above normal by\nlate week. By the weekend, temperatures begin to gradually \ndecrease each day.",
